&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<font face="Comic Sans MS" size="4" color="#FF6600">
AIMS and THEMES</font>
</center>
Historically, the theory of Hamiltonian Systems, besides having grown
out of concrete applications, has generated many new mathematical ideas
and theories.<br>

In recent times, reflecting a broader trend in Mathematics, major 
advances related to Hamiltonian Systems have been prompted by the 
(re)unification of seemingly disparate theories and point of views.
<br>

For this reason we think that in this very exciting times
it is important that there is a workshop that brings together some of the
people who have contributed to the developments that  
took place in the last several years.
<br>
The works will revolve around the following themes:  Symplectic
    topology. Bifurcations. Statistical properties. Hamiltonian
    methods in PDE. Variational methods and Arnold diffusion. Interval
    exchanges and renormalization. Numerical methods, Transport
    properties and applications.  
